Description: network scanner that shows what your servers expose
 tcpcat scans your servers from the outside and tells what they expose, why
 it matters and how to fix it. It is written in Go and intended for
 authorized security assessments and network administration.
 .
 Main features are:
   * TCP SYN/connect and UDP port scanning, with ARP or ping-based host
     discovery
   * service and version detection, including TLS details, and OS
     fingerprinting
   * CVE correlation against an embedded offline database, Google OSV or
     the Vulners API
   * an offline AI (--ai, --ai-deep, "tcpcat ai") that says what to fix,
     why and how, verifies its findings with read-only probes and tells
     what blocked the scan
   * JSON, SARIF, JSONL audit log and baseline-comparison reports
   * scope file and a conservative "safe-production" profile for approved
     engagements
   * optional eBPF/AF_XDP engine on Linux (--ebpf) that only redirects the
     replies to its own probes, leaving all other traffic to the kernel
   * exposure analysis: "tcpcat inventory" records which process, systemd
     unit, container or pod listens on each port, and "tcpcat explain"
     classifies each port as seen from outside and writes the fix
   * segmentation policy checks, signed evidence bundles and passive
     (eBPF tracepoint) discovery
